y re-opened the museum in 2012.","wpu":"National Maritime Museum of Ireland","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/e/e3/Mariners-church-dun-laoghaire-restored.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"The Oratory","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":53.292526,"lo":-6.139952,"d":428,"w":"https://www.dunlaoghaire.ie/ttd/the-oratory/"},{"n":"Crimean War cannon","g":"sights","k":"monument","la":53.292933,"lo":-6.129449,"d":434},{"n":"Haddington House","g":"sights","k":"hotel","la":53.292026,"lo":-6.130487,"d":446,"w":"https://haddingtonhouse.ie/","ph":"+353 1 280 1810","wifi":1},{"n":"Geographic Pointer","g":"sights","k":"viewpoint","la":53.293855,"lo":-6.12847,"d":452},{"n":"Dublin Way View Finder","g":"sights","k":"monument","la":53.293853,"lo":-6.128469,"d":452},{"n":"King Laoghaire","g":"sights","k":"monument","la":53.296435,"lo":-6.142696,"d":535},{"n":"Mulgrave Lodge","g":"sights","k":"guest_house","la":53.289668,"lo":-6.136897,"d":606,"w":"https://www.mulgravelodge.com/","ph":"+353 1 280 1664","wifi":1},{"n":"Bandstand","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":53.289903,"lo":-6.128525,"d":711},{"n":"Monkstown Castle","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":53.288995,"lo":-6.150511,"d":1228,"w":"https://www.archaeology.ie/MonumentoftheMonth/MonkstownCastleMonkstownCoDublin/","wp":"en:Monkstown Castle","ds":"Monkstown (Irish: Baile na Manach), historically known as Carrickbrennan (Irish: Carraig Bhraonáin), is a suburb of Dublin, Ireland, approximately 10 km (6 mi) south-east of Dublin city centre. It is within the jurisdiction of Dún Laoghaire–Rathdown County Council. It is on the coast, between Blackrock and Dún Laoghaire while also bordering Sallynoggin and Deansgrange inland.","wpu":"Monkstown, Dublin","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/ee/Monkstown_Dublin.JPG/960px-Monkstown_Dublin.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Sandycove Beach","g":"sights","k":"beach","la":53.288565,"lo":-6.114499,"d":1539,"w":"https://www.beaches.ie/find-a-beach/#/beach/IEEABWC090_0000_0050"},{"n":"Forty Foot","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":53.289486,"lo":-6.113699,"d":1542,"w":"https://www.beaches.ie/find-a-beach/#/beach/IEEABWC090_0000_0040","ds":"The Forty Foot (Irish: Cladach an Daichead Troigh) is a promontory on the southern tip of Dublin Bay at Sandycove, County Dublin, Ireland, from which people have been swimming in the Irish Sea all year round for some 250 years.","wpu":"Forty Foot","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/55/Forty_Foot_2008.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"James Joyce Tower and Museum","g":"sights","k":"museum","la":53.288669,"lo":-6.113609,"d":1586,"h":"Mo-Su 10:00-18:00","w":"http://jamesjoycetower.com/","ph":"+353 1 280 9265","wp":"en:James Joyce Tower and Museum","ds":"The James Joyce Tower and Museum is a Martello tower in Sandycove, Dublin, where James Joyce spent six nights in 1904. The opening scenes of his 1922 novel Ulysses take place here, and the tower is a place of pilgrimage for Joyce enthusiasts, especially on Bloomsday. Admission is free.","wpu":"James Joyce Tower and Museum","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/3f/James_Joyce_Tower_01.JPG/960px-James_Joyce_Tower_01.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Seapoint","g":"sights","k":"beach","la":53.297211,"lo":-6.158681,"d":1592,"w":"https://www.beaches.ie/find-a-beach/#/beach/IEEABWC090_0000_0100","ds":"Seapoint (Irish: Rinn na Mara) is a small seafront area between Blackrock and Monkstown in Dublin in the Dún Laoghaire–Rathdown area. It is best known for its beach and bathing areas, beside a Martello Tower on the Dublin Bay seashore. The beach currently has an International Blue flag award and a national Green flag award.","wpu":"Seapoint","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/85/D%26KR_view_1840.jpg/960px-D%26KR_view_1840.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"IADT Sign","g":"sights","k":"monument","la":53.281357,"lo":-6.15382,"d":1966},{"n":"Ernest Walton","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":53.283404,"lo":-6.167358,"d":2507},{"n":"1916 Monument","g":"sights","k":"monument","la":53.281784,"lo":-6.165958,"d":2528},{"n":"John McCormack","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":53.282533,"lo":-6.167679,"d":2576},{"n":"John A. Costelloe","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":53.282762,"lo":-6.167962,"d":2579,"wp":"en:John A. Costello","ds":"John Aloysius Costello (20 June 1891 – 5 January 1976) was an Irish Fine Gael politician who served as Taoiseach from 1948 to 1951 and from 1954 to 1957. He was leader of the opposition from 1951 to 1954 and from 1957 to 1959 and attorney general from 1926 to 1932. He served as a Teachta Dála (TD) from 1933 to 1943 and from 1944 to 1969.","wpu":"John A. Costello","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/3/37/John_A._Costello%2C_1948.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"Todd Andrews","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":53.282721,"lo":-6.167926,"d":2579},{"n":"Hermann Get","g":"sights","k":"attraction","la":53.282798,"lo":-6.168892,"d":2629,"wp":"en:Hermann Görtz","ds":"Hermann Görtz (also anglicised as Goertz; 15 November 1890 – 23 May 1947) was a German spy in Britain and Ireland before and during World War II, liaising with the Irish Republican Army (IRA). After the war, he committed suicide rather than be deported from Ireland to Germany.","wpu":"Hermann Görtz"},{"n":"AIB","g":"essentials","k":"atm","la":53.294167,"lo":-6.133485,"d":136,"h":"24/7"},{"n":"Royal St George Yacht Club","g":"essentials","k":"wifi","la":53.294476,"lo":-6.132278,"d":190,"w":"https://www.rsgyc.ie/","ph":"+353 1 280 1811","wifi":1},{"n":"St. Michael's Hospital","g":"essentials","k":"hospital","la":53.29413,"lo":-6.138026,"d":223,"h":"Mo-