d the Mailboat Pier.","wpu":"Dún Laoghaire Harbour","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/21/Dun_Laoghaire_Aerial.jpg/960px-Dun_Laoghaire_Aerial.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Carrickbrennan Churchyard","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":53.289901,"lo":-6.150617,"d":1182,"ds":"Carrickbrennan Churchyard (Irish: Reilig Charraig Bhraonáin) located on Carrickbrennan Road, Monkstown, Dublin, Ireland is a graveyard that can still be seen today, but is no longer in use. It is notable as the burial place of many people who perished in local maritime disasters.","wpu":"Carrickbrennan Churchyard","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/de/Carrickbrennan_Churchyard.JPG/960px-Carrickbrennan_Churchyard.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Lambert Puppet Theatre","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":53.294534,"lo":-6.154756,"d":1314,"ds":"The Lambert Puppet Theatre & Museum was a puppet theatre located in Monkstown, Dublin, Ireland. It was a family-run business established in 1972 by Eugene Lambert, and hosted an international puppet festival annually. It produced children's television series on Radio Telefís Éireann such as Wanderly Wagon.","wpu":"Lambert Puppet Theatre","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/99/LambertPuppetTheatre.jpg/960px-LambertPuppetTheatre.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Bullock Harbour","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":53.285278,"lo":-6.107778,"d":2107,"ds":"Bullock Harbour or Bulloch Harbour (Irish: Cuan Bhlóic) is a small working harbour located near the heritage town of Dalkey on the southeast coast of Dublin Bay in Ireland. The current harbour quay and walls were constructed of local granite in the early 19th century where previously a rocky inlet had provided a natural harbour.","wpu":"Bullock Harbour","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/70/Bulloch_Harbour.jpg/960px-Bulloch_Harbour.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Friends Burial Ground, Dublin","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":53.295351,"lo":-6.171705,"d":2439,"ds":"The Friends Burial Ground (Irish: Reilig Chumann na gCairde), also called Temple Hill Burial Ground or the Friends Sleeping Place is a Quaker burial ground located at Temple Hill, Blackrock, Dublin. It opened in 1860 and is one of two Quaker burial grounds in Dublin, the other being at Cork Street.","wpu":"Friends Burial Ground, Dublin","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Friends_Burial_Ground%2C_Temple_Hill.JPG/960px-Friends_Burial_Ground%2C_Temple_Hill.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Archbold's Castle","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":53.27789,"lo":-6.106422,"d":2688,"ds":"Archbold's Castle is a castle and a National Monument in Dalkey, Ireland.","wpu":"Archbold's Castle","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/b/b1/Archibolds_Castle_Dalkey.JPG/960px-Archibolds_Castle_Dalkey.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"St. John the Baptist, Blackrock","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":53.300077,"lo":-6.174958,"d":2714,"ds":"St. John the Baptist, Blackrock is a Roman Catholic church in the parish of Blackrock, Ireland. The church is still in use and named after the Saint John the Baptist.","wpu":"St. John the Baptist, Blackrock","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/23/St._John_The_Baptist%2C_Blackrock.JPG/960px-St._John_The_Baptist%2C_Blackrock.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Dalkey","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":53.276,"lo":-6.104,"d":2951,"ds":"Dalkey ( DAW-kee; Irish: Deilginis, meaning 'thorn island') is a suburban town in Dún Laoghaire–Rathdown county southeast of Dublin, Ireland. It was founded as a Viking settlement and became a port in the Middle Ages. According to chronicler John Clyn (c.1286–c.1349), it was one of the ports through which the plague entered Ireland in the mid-14th century.","wpu":"Dalkey","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e4/DalkeyWMurphy.jpg/960px-DalkeyWMurphy.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Killiney Castle","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":53.2693,"lo":-6.1133,"d":3201,"ds":"Killiney Castle, also known as Mount Malpas, Rocksborough, or Loftus Hill, and now known as Fitzpatrick Castle Hotel, is an 18th-century manor house near Killiney in County Dublin, Ireland. The castle was converted into a hotel, which has been open since 1971.","wpu":"Killiney Castle","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/5/50/Killiney_Castle.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"},{"n":"St. Andrew's, Blackrock","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":53.301567,"lo":-6.184525,"d":3371,"ds":"St. Andrew's, Blackrock is a Presbyterian church on Mount Merrion Avenue, Blackrock, Dublin. The Blackrock parish was established in 1895 and the church was opened in 1899.","wpu":"St. Andrew's, Blackrock","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d9/St_Andrews_Church_Blackrock.jpg/960px-St_Andrews_Church_Blackrock.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Killiney","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":53.2651,"lo":-6.1137,"d":3613,"ds":"Killiney (Irish: Cill Iníon Léinín, meaning 'church of the daughters of Léinín') is an affluent coastal suburb on the southside of Dublin, Ireland. It lies south of Dalkey, east and northeast of Ballybrack and Sallynoggin and north of Shankill, in the local government area of Dún Laoghaire–Rathdown within County Dublin.","wpu":"Killiney","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/0/0f/Killiney_Beach_-_2025-06-21.jpg/960px-Killiney_Beach_-_2025-06-21.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"St. Philip and St. James Church, Booterstown","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":53.300996,"lo":-6.191237,"d":3796,"ds":"St. Philip and St. James Church, Booterstown is a church of the Church of Ireland located in Booterstown, Dublin.","wpu":"St. Philip and St. James Church, Booterstown","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/3/36/St._Philip_and_St_James_Church_Booterstown.jpg/960px-St._Philip_and_St_James_Church_Booterstown.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Manderley Castle","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":53.2627,"lo":-6.113,"d":3878,"ds":"Manderley Castle (Irish: Caisléan Mhanderley), formerly \"Victoria Castle\" and \"Ayesha Castle,\" is a large castellated Irish mansion built in Victorian style, in Killiney, County Dublin, Ireland. It has been owned by the musician Enya since 1997.","wpu":"Manderley Castle","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/c/c7/Enya_House.jpg/960px-Enya_House.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},{"n":"Dalkey Hill","g":"sights","k":"landmark","la":53.266667,"lo":-6.1,"d":3916,"ds":"Dalkey Hill ( DAW-kee; Irish: Cnoc Dheilginse) is the northernmost of the two hills which form the southern boundary of Dublin Bay (the other being Killiney Hill). Dalkey Hill is 140 metres high and has views over the surrounding areas : Dublin to the northwest; the Irish Sea and the mountains of Wales (on a clear day) to the east and southeast; and Bray Head and the Wicklow Mountains to the south.","wpu":"Dalkey Hill","img":"https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/8/8d/Dalkey_hill.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail_unscaled"}],"about":{"ds":"Dublin is the capital and largest city of Ireland. Situated on Dublin Bay at the mouth of the River Liffey, it is in the province of Leinster, and is bordered on the south by the Dublin Mountains, part of the Wicklow Mountains range. Dublin is the largest city by population on the island of Ireland; at the 2022 census, the city council area had a population of 592,713, while the city, including suburbs, had a population of 1,263,219, and County Dublin had a population of 1,501,500.","wpu":"Dublin","img":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/92/Dublin_-_aerial_-_2025-07-07_01.jpg/960px-Dublin_-_aerial_-_2025-07-07_01.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail"},"wk":"2026-10-18"}